ie Challenger\n\nAvailable as\n\n[Input] Paperback\n\n[Input] eBook\n\n[Input] Audiobook\n\nPublished\n    3 Feb 2022\n\nISBN\n    9781786895752\n\nRRP\n    £10.99\n\nExtent\n    272 pp\n\nDimensions\n    129×198mm\n\nPublished\n    4 Feb 2021\n\nISBN\n    9781786895745\n\nRRP\n    £8.79\n\nExtent\n    272 pp\n\nPublished\n    4 Feb 2021\n\nISBN\n    9781786895813\n\nRRP\n    £25\n\nDuration\n    7 hrs 25 min\n\nRead by Melanie Challenger\n\n## In a few words\n\nCombining popular science, history and moral philosophy, this is a wide-ranging and radical take on the human story and what it means for us today\n\n## In a few words\n\nCombining popular science, history and moral philosophy, this is a wide-ranging and radical take on the human story and what it means for us today\n\n## In a few words\n\nCombining nature writing, history and moral philosophy, this is a wide-ranging and radical new take on the human story and what it means for us today\n\n> The best critique of the myth of human exceptionalism I have read. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJudgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases (Chapter 1) - Judgment under Uncertainty (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/judgment-under-uncertainty/judgment-under-uncertainty-heuristics-and-biases/9086E911E7585FE81DE70A4FA96AEA13)\nciteturn350search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.5 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; \n\n# 1 - Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  05 May 2013\n\nBy\n\nAmos Tversky  and\n\nDaniel Kahneman\n\nEdited by\n\nDaniel Kahneman ,\n\nPaul Slovic  and\n\nAmos Tversky\n\nShow author details\n\nBook contents\n\n  * Frontmatter\n  * Contents\n  * List of contributors\n  * Preface\n  * Part I Introduction\n  * 1 Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases\n  * Part II Representativeness\n  * Part III Causality and attribution\n  * Part IV Availability\n  * Part V Covariation and control\n  * Part VI Overconfidence\n  * Part VII Multistage evaluation\n  * Part VIII Corrective procedures\n  * Part IX Risk perception\n  * Part X Postscript\n  * References\n  * Index\n\n## Summary\n\nMany decisions are based on beliefs concerning the likelihood of uncertain events such as the outcome of an election, the guilt of a defendant, or the future value of the dollar. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJudgment under Uncertainty (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/judgment-under-uncertainty/6F9E814794E08EC43D426E480A4B412C/listing)\nciteturn350search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n  * Edited by Daniel Kahneman, Paul Slovic, Amos Tversky\n  * Book:\n    Judgment under Uncertainty\nPublished online:\n    05 May 2013\nPrint publication:\n    30 April 1982, pp 463-490\n  *     * Chapter\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n    * Export citation\n  * View extract\n\n#### Summary\n\nPeople respond to the hazards they perceive. If their perceptions are faulty, efforts at personal, public, and environmental protection are likely to be misdirected. For some hazards, such as motor vehicle accidents, extensive statistical data are readily available. For other familiar activities, such as the consumption of alcohol and tobacco, assessment of risk requires complex epidemiological and experimental studies. However, even when statistical data are plentiful, the “hard” facts can only go so far toward developing policy. At some point, human judgment is needed to interpret the findings and determine their relevance.\nWe believe that three such difficulties are clearly visible now, and we believe that attempts to solve each of the problems will pay dividends in terms of extending our understanding both of how people infer and of how they ought to infer.\n\n* * *\n\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### Part X - Postscript\n\n  * Edited by Daniel Kahneman, Paul Slovic, Amos Tversky\n  * Book:\n    Judgment under Uncertainty\nPublished online:\n    05 May 2013\nPrint publication:\n    30 April 1982, pp 491-492\n  *     * Chapter\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n    * Export citation\n\n* * *\n\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### Index\n\n  * Edited by Daniel Kahneman, Paul Slovic, Amos Tversky\n  * Book:\n    Judgment under Uncertainty\nPublished online:\n    05 May 2013\nPrint publication:\n    30 April 1982, pp 553-555\n  *     * Chapter\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n    * Export citation\n\n* * *\n\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### 2 - Belief in the law of small numbers\n\n  *     * By Amos Tversky, Stanford University, Daniel Kahneman, University of British Columbia\n  * Edited by Daniel Kahneman, Paul Slovic, Amos Tversky\n  * Book:\n    Judgment under Uncertainty\nPublished online:\n    05 May 2013\nPrint publication:\n    30 April 1982, pp 23-31\n  *     * Chapter\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n    * Export citation\n  * View extract\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to Be Animal: A New History of What It Means to Be Human | Microcosm Publishing (https://microcosmpublishing.com/catalog/books/273)\nciteturn350search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; \n\n# How to Be Animal: A New History of What It Means to Be Human\n\nby Melanie Challenger Author\n\nIn this fascinating new book from author Melanie Challenger, explore what it means to be a human, and more importantly, what it means to be an animal. Challenger argues that a critical aspect of the trajectory of human development was shaped by our confrontation with the fact of our animality. Even as we discovered new principles of science and made observations about the world that other animals had and could not, we struggled with our instincts and animality in such a way as to spur our desire to shape and control the world even further. Persuasively argued, and a fascinating look at the whole of human history through a new lens, How to Be Animal is a must read for the biologically and anthropologically inclined.\n\nThis combination of biology, genetics, zoology, evolutionary psychology and philosophy offers a wide-ranging take on an important question. Why do we has humans resist the idea that we are animals? As a species we pat ourselves on the back for our intelligence, but how smart are we if we don't even admit we are animals? Author Melanie Challenger tells the story of what it is to be human and the existential struggle we face from facing our own animal faces. Challenger traces the history of this attitude from the agrarian and industrial revolutions through the age of internet. How to Be Animal is a reappraisal of humanity and also a defense of what it means to be an animal.\n\n(This title may contain a sharpie mark on the top or bottom edge and may show mild signs of shelfwear.)\n\n  * $9.98 $19.95\n\n    * 272 pages (14 oz)\n    * 5.45\" x 8.25\" x 0.7\"\n    * ISBN 9780735238121\n    * Publisher: Allen Lane\n  * $17.00\n\n    * 272 pages (8 oz)\n    * 5.5\" x 8.2\" x 0.7\"\n    * ISBN 9780143134350\n    * Publisher: Allen Lane\n\n### Subjects\n\n  * Sociology\n  * Nature\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNEW TITLES FOR AUTUMN 2018 (https://www.urbanomic.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Urbanomic-Autumn-2018-p1-web.pdf)\nciteturn350search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.0 years ago; Crawled: 8.0 years ago; \nNEW TITLES FOR AUTUMN 2018\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nBook cover image for “Intelligence and Spirit,” shown as a paperback lying at an angle on a light gray background; the cover is mostly white and gray with the title visible on the top face.\n</visual_element>\n\nIntelligence and Spirit  \nREZA NEGARESTANI\n\nThrough stunning readings of Kant, Hegel, and especially Plato, Reza Negarestani shows that the freedom proper to the subject and the power proper to the object are unintelligible if separated from the movement of an intelligence that is both unnatured and unnaturing.  \n— Ray Brassier, author of *Nihil Unbound*\n\nA philosophical tour de force that reintroduces Hegel’s Geist to Kant in order to arrive at a neutral conception of intelligence that accommodates AI and future programs of intelligence.  \n— Adam Berg, author of *Phenomenalism, Phenomenology, and the Question of Time*\n\nThe history of philosophy conceived as the elaboration of a program for artificial general intelligence; intelligence understood as the impersonal and collective evolution of a thought constructing itself according to a view from nowhere and nowhen.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBooks — Melanie Challenger (https://melaniechallenger.com/books)\nciteturn350search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; \n\n04 · Books\n\n# The books\n\nMy first co-edited book, Stolen Voices, was an epistolary history of twentieth-century conflict. My first solo-authored book, On Extinction, was a loose environmental history of the Anthropocene, focusing in particular on the nostalgic impulse.\n\nMy second book, How to Be Animal, in which I write more explicitly on my ideas, is primarily diagnostic: humans are animals whose awareness of vulnerability and death repeatedly drives them to imagine their real identity somewhere outside the body.\n\nMy third book, Alive, is a positive ontology, arguing that living bodies are not passive matter but self-maintaining, purposive centres of activity, norms, and agency.\n\nI am now working on a book that narrates an encounter with a ghost in my youth that challenges my philosophy.\n\nIn progress\n\n## Our Beloved Ghosts\n\nMaking sense of a spectre.\n\nHow a haunting in my youth is challenging the fundamental ideas in my work.\n\n## Books\n\n2006 — present\n\n  1. In progress\n\n### Our Beloved Ghosts\n\nMaking sense of a spectre\n\nHow a haunting in my youth is challenging the fundamental ideas in my work.\n\n  2. 2026\n\n### Alive\n\nThe hidden intelligence of the living world\n\nA positive ontology arguing that living bodies are not passive matter but self-maintaining, purposive centres of activity, norms, and agency.\n\n  3. 2021\n\n### How to Be Animal\n\nWhat it means to be human\n\nA diagnostic work that argues humans are animals whose awareness of vulnerability and death repeatedly drives them to imagine their real identity somewhere outside the body.\n\n  4. 2011\n\n### On Extinction\n\nHow We Became Estranged from Nature\n\nA loose environmental history of the Anthropocene, focusing in particular on the nostalgic impulse and our estrangement from the natural world.\n\n  5. 2006\n\n### Stolen Voices\n\nYoung People's War Diaries, from World War I to Iraq\n\nAn epistolary history of twentieth-century conflict, told through the war diaries of children and young people from the First World War to Iraq.\n\n## Selected editions\n\nImage: Original cover of On Extinction\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to Be Animal by Melanie Challenger | Penguin Random House Canada (https://www.penguinrandomhouse.ca/books/606910/how-to-be-animal-by-melanie-challenger/9780735242609)\nciteturn350search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n\n# How to Be Animal\n\n## A New History of What It Means to Be Human\n\nAuthor  Melanie Challenger\n\nImage: How to Be Animal\n\nAdd to Goodreads\n\nWhat makes us human, and why are we so sure we're different from other animals?\nHumans are the most inquisitive, emotional, imaginative, aggressive, and baffling animals on the planet. But how well do we really know ourselves? How to Be Animal rewrites the remarkable human story and argues that at the heart of our psychology is a profound struggle with being animal.\nMost of our effects on the planet are the consequences of technological improvements and advances in our understanding of natural mechanisms. But why did this cognitive and technological edge come about in the first place and what kind of being has it made us? In How to Be Animal, Challenger brilliantly argues that this dizzying trajectory is the result of… [Button: Keep Reading]\n\nRead an Excerpt Listen to a Clip\n\nPaperback\n\n###### $22.95\n\nMarch 23, 2021\n\nBook Details\n\nAbout Melanie Challenger\n\nMELANIE CHALLENGER writes, researches, and broadcasts on the history of ideas, the history and philosophy of science, and the relationship between humans and the living world. She is the author of How to Be Animal: What it Means to Be Human, among other works, and host of the podcast The Psychosphere. She is also an award-winning poet and librettist for opera and oratorios, and a National Geographic Explorer. Melanie is internationally active in bioethics, co-director of Animals in the Room, and Vice President of the RSPCA.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCAMBRIDGECambridge University Press (https://assets.cambridge.org/97805212/84141/frontmatter/9780521284141_frontmatter.pdf)\nciteturn350search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; \nCAMBRIDGECambridge University Press\n978-0-521-28414-1 — Judgment under Uncertainty\nEdited by Daniel Kahneman , Paul Slovic , Amos Tversky\nFrontmatter\nMore InformationCAMBRIDGE\nUNIVERSITY PRESSOne Liberty Plaza, 20th Floor, New York, NY 10006, USACambridge University Press is part of the University of Cambridge.It furthers the University’s mission by disseminating knowledge in the pursuit of\neducation, learning and research at the highest international levels of excellence.www.cambridge.org\nInformation on this title: www.cambridge.org/9780521284141© Cambridge University 